WEBVTT

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:04.240
Oh, it's beautiful. Really beautiful.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:07.460
You know, we could build right on this spot.

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:09.990
Landowners.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:14.410
To our future home.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:21.310
Our future home.

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:29.970
You have some brie in that bed?

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.890
Yeah. Here, hold this.

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:43.980
Oh, maybe I left the knife in the car.

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:47.330
Oh, all right. Well, you wait here. I'll go get it.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:47.810
Okay.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:56.440
Michelle?

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:56.980
Yeah?

00:00:59.060 --> 00:00:59.960
Did you come here for a sec?

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.900
What is it, honey?

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:06.500
Which way did we come?

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:12.030
Oh, I really don't know.

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:06.050
Okay, Sicilians, that's the end of another broadcasting day.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:09.810
Before we happy trails, we've got a few Founder's Day updates.

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:14.700
Hayden Keyes still has some of his limited edition prints of our founding mothers,

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.700
Sicily and Roslyn, for sale, each one individually signed by the artist himself.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:24.460
And Games Commissioner Connie Grippo warns if she doesn't get two more sign-ups

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:28.420
for the Jack and Joe treetopping, the event's going to be canceled for lack of participation.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:29.980
One more reminder.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.760
Reminder to us all, show your pride, wear the sock.

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:37.630
What's up with all these socks everywhere?

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.520
It's for Founder's Day, the reason why we're here.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:43.060
A sock?

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.910
When Cicely and Rosalind drove up from Montana,

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.130
Rosalind lost one of her socks,

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:52.770
and she hated wearing socks that didn't match,

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:55.130
so she stopped here and bought a new pair.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:58.200
So you're telling me this entire town

00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.820
was founded because of a sock?

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:01.520
That's a story.

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:02.530
You want one?

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:05.980
Yeah, sure.

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:06.550
Whatever.

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:15.280
It looks like nobody's been here in 30 years.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.140
Hey, I don't see anything.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:25.840
Well, what are we gonna do?

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:28.700
Well, B&E used to be my thing.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:29.080
You mind?

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:39.120
Go ahead. It's nobody was home when you broke in, huh?

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:39.540
Yeah.

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:57.260
Vix, got it?

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:57.840
Yeah.

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:02.350
Look at that.

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:15.820
Rosalind Sisley is on Model T.

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:25.040
Another year, another Founders Day.

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:27.260
Boy, they go faster and faster, don't they?

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:29.880
How many is that for me?

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.800
Now, let's see, 27?

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:33.560
Uh-uh.

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:35.230
No, 28.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:39.480
Cool pick, Sage.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:41.400
Boss redo, huh?

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.200
The way Holling spiffed up the nursery.

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:45.920
Oh, yeah.

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:49.250
Super dad.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:56.030
Hey, what'd you do that for?

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:58.040
You just got it built up.

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:06.980
That's kids for you. Build it up, knock it down. Build it up, knock it down.

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:08.140
Yeah, I guess.

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:13.330
I don't know how you have the patience. Kids bore me.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:17.000
Before I had Randy, I used to feel the same way. I didn't care much for them.

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:20.450
But I tell you now, Maurice, I'd like a dozen.

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:24.210
Well, if I ever had offspring, I'd want them ready-made.

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:26.990
Boy, about 10 or 11 years old, hunting age.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.840
Randy, I guess you had that coming.

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:43.560
Phil, we can't build the kind of house we've been talking about.

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:44.460
What do you mean?

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:50.630
Well, 3,000 square feet at $100 a square foot, that's $300,000.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.280
Yeah, well, we still have $15,000.

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:57.110
We put the land up as collateral, we don't have enough to qualify for loans.

00:05:57.940 --> 00:05:59.740
We don't really have $15,000.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.840
Honey, we forgot to take into account the legal fees for the dissolution of your partnership.

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:06.300
Well, how much could that be?

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:13.160
Well, we met with Bernie four times in September, plus the phone conversations and all the papers he's drawing up.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:15.340
I bet we're looking at seven, eight thousand dollars.

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:19.230
Well, that still leaves us...

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:23.660
No, it doesn't. Visa bill. Three thousand four hundred and seventy three dollars.

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:24.520
What?

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:29.160
We charged our moving costs in that refrigerator we bought for you folks, remember?

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.900
If we build small, we can always add on, right?

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:41.380
What was that?

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:55.280
This.

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:56.540
Came this way, did he?

00:06:57.810 --> 00:06:58.250
Walt?

00:06:58.520 --> 00:06:59.580
Who? Who came this way?

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:06.670
Big one moose why would he attack our car it didn't attack your car it tried to mount it

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:12.350
Mount it mate I know it's not the season for it but sometimes the scent of these Japanese

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:20.650
radios mimics the natural pheromones drives them wild don't worry won't get away from me I'll let

00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:21.510
you have his rack

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:31.210
Ruthann, there you are.

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:35.170
I was wondering if I could just take off for a couple hours this afternoon.

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:37.930
I told Lester Haynes I'd help him with the Chamber of Commerce float.

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:39.010
I guess so.

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:40.750
What's their theme, monsieur?

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:44.590
Oh, Sicily, Paris of the North.

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:51.070
It's gonna be an Eiffel Tower with black Dutch tulips perched on a snowball of daylilies.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:53.220
Sounds lovely.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:58.060
Why don't you go over to the store and get a sack of gingerbread sock cookies for the boys?

00:07:58.500 --> 00:07:59.660
Oh, hey, thanks.

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:03.380
Hello, Holly.

00:08:03.890 --> 00:08:04.410
All right.

00:08:05.450 --> 00:08:09.000
Mind if I visit with Miranda for a few minutes?

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:10.110
I guess not.

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:12.580
Hello.

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:15.730
Randy.

00:08:17.090 --> 00:08:19.700
Not so.

00:08:21.050 --> 00:08:21.390
That way.

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:24.080
He used to pull my ear just like that.

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:26.700
My Uncle Elby.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.190
Miranda is my Uncle Elby.

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:37.650
Maurice, you OD on the Johnny Walker?

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:40.470
No, no, I'm cold sober, Shelly.

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:45.840
It's not just the ear thing, it's also the look in the eyes.

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:47.700
I know it anyway.

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:54.050
What exactly are you trying to say, Maurice?

00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:56.930
Reincarnation, Holly.

00:08:58.670 --> 00:08:59.970
Don't ask me how, but...

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:03.060
Some way, somehow,

00:09:05.150 --> 00:09:08.510
your Miranda is my Uncle Elby come back again.

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:12.700
How about that?

00:10:16.990 --> 00:10:20.670
We thought if we could sell off a couple of acres, maybe even seven or eight, we could

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:22.510
make enough money to qualify for our loan.

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:26.780
Since you know the market, we'd be happy to let you handle the sale.

00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:28.990
Yeah?

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.520
Michelle and I thought this would be a pretty desirable parcel.

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:33.980
I mean, you got that little lake over there.

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:35.650
You know, you got the mountain.

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:39.040
And you tell me there's a waterfall in the summertime.

00:10:41.620 --> 00:10:44.850
Yeah, uh, Phil, I'm just a little puzzled.

00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:49.090
I mean, why do you think someone would want this piece of property?

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:50.400
Why?

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:55.580
It's beautiful, pristine.

00:10:57.280 --> 00:10:57.680
Well, yeah.

00:10:58.890 --> 00:10:59.960
Well, who wouldn't?

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:09.160
want this kind of piece of property. Well, Phil, look, see, you're way out, and there's not a decent

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:18.160
road, and you're not on the grid. You're joking, right? Come on, Maggie, people in LA would kill

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:23.980
for a piece of property like this. We can put an ad in Hollywood Reporter. All right, okay, I'll try.

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:25.780
Okay, good, good.

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:59.420
me out of here. Okay, okay, okay. Okay, you're fine. You're fine.

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:03.340
You're fine.

00:12:04.610 --> 00:12:05.570
I can't move.

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:06.630
Okay, yes, you can.

00:12:06.950 --> 00:12:07.810
Yes, you can.

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:08.950
Okay, try to take a deep breath.

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:09.570
I can't breathe.

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:10.510
Relax.

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:11.630
Relax, relax.

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:12.690
Can you turn around?

00:12:12.970 --> 00:12:15.010
Look, I know my way around here really, really well.

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:15.610
It's okay.

00:12:15.850 --> 00:12:16.870
Breathe, take a deep breath, relax.

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:18.430
Try to relax.

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:19.510
Okay?

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:20.190
Deep breath.

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:29.720
Dude, I just feel so ridiculous.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:32.360
I'm sorry, Maggie. I'm so sorry.

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:33.580
Oh, listen, it's all right.

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:34.740
I'm so embarrassed.

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:37.220
Don't be embarrassed.

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:40.900
God, I haven't had an anxiety attack like that in years.

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:42.740
Not since the audit.

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:48.830
Take the volume, honey, okay?

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:53.910
Listen, if you guys don't need me, I'll just be going, okay?

00:12:54.310 --> 00:12:55.690
Thank you, Maggie. I'm so sorry.

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:56.720
It's okay.

00:12:57.380 --> 00:12:58.260
I'll walk you to the door.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:12:59.900
You'll be okay for six?

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:08.210
Well, thanks again, Maggie. I really appreciate it.

00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:10.510
Look, if you need to see a doctor, I'll be happy to find you.

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:10.870
Yeah, okay.

00:13:11.290 --> 00:13:14.910
Um, Maggie, was it you who gave me this nice parka and boots?

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:16.260
Mm, no.

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:18.730
Oh. Any idea who might have?

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:21.200
No. Looks warm, though.

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:22.020
Yeah.

00:13:23.260 --> 00:13:24.080
Okay, well, thanks.

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:25.220
Okay, yeah. Let me know.

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:25.740
I will.

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:26.300
Bye.

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:26.700
All right, bye.

00:13:35.630 --> 00:13:36.230
How are you feeling?

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:38.440
Better.

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:42.650
You sure it was an anxiety attack?

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:45.000
Oh, textbook presentation.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:50.450
Palpitations, tremors, overwhelming sense of terror.

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:54.620
I wonder what caused it.

00:13:56.820 --> 00:13:58.440
I think it was the woods.

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:04.160
The woods? You mean like agoraphobia?

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:09.020
No, worse than that. Nature. Naturophobia.

00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:11.020
You're afraid of nature?

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:18.090
Huge trees, mountains, all those animal noises.

00:14:18.150 --> 00:14:21.410
It was almost as if the entire ecosystem were trying to devour me.

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:22.030
Oh, Phil.

00:14:24.110 --> 00:14:29.250
But, Michelle, we just bought 60 acres of nature.

00:14:30.700 --> 00:14:33.700
Phil, honey, I'm sure it's just a passing thing.

00:14:34.100 --> 00:14:35.560
And it probably wasn't nature.

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:38.480
It was probably something that you really weren't even aware of.

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:41.760
You know, all the times that we went skiing in Vail.

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:44.970
This never happened. You didn't have this problem.

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:47.220
Yeah, yeah, maybe you're right.

00:14:48.650 --> 00:14:51.280
A lot of anxiety is free-floating.

00:14:52.030 --> 00:14:54.750
Maybe it's not nature or the land.

00:14:56.310 --> 00:14:57.690
Maybe it's my blood sugar.

00:14:58.130 --> 00:14:59.650
Right, yeah.

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:03.670
Better? Yeah, yeah.

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:10.840
Phil, let me ask you something.

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:19.060
How would you feel if someone gave you some clothes anonymously?

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:20.030
What?

00:15:21.500 --> 00:15:24.540
This afternoon there was a box on the front porch and it was addressed to me.

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:27.980
There was an old parka in it and a pair of boots.

00:15:30.090 --> 00:15:30.970
And this was in it, too.

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:33.360
Thought you could use these.

00:15:34.830 --> 00:15:35.650
Pretty low, huh?

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:36.560
Low?

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:41.500
They think that I can't see through this?

00:15:45.950 --> 00:15:47.410
Anonymous gifts?

00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:49.390
Anonymous suggestions?

00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:50.410
They're hostile.

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:53.300
The park is a comment.

00:15:54.310 --> 00:15:56.810
It says, I don't like what you wear.

00:15:56.810 --> 00:15:58.830
I want you to dress like the rest of us.

00:15:59.320 --> 00:15:59.980
Well, no.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:03.220
Michelle, you are cold a lot of the time.

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:05.300
You know, it couldn't hurt you to dress more warmly.

00:16:05.380 --> 00:16:05.800
Maybe...

00:16:05.800 --> 00:16:09.320
Well, Phil, my comfort level is nobody's business but mine.

00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:11.560
I'm gonna find out who did this.

00:16:25.250 --> 00:16:26.810
Little angel is asleep.

00:16:27.330 --> 00:16:27.690
Already?

00:16:31.630 --> 00:16:32.450
Maurice.

00:16:34.720 --> 00:16:35.540
Hello, Holly.

00:16:36.130 --> 00:16:36.610
Shelly.

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:38.960
I brought a few trinkets here for Miranda.

00:16:39.140 --> 00:16:39.560
Is she about?

00:16:39.740 --> 00:16:40.720
It's past her bedtime.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:42.740
She's in dreamland, Maurice.

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:43.220
Oh.

00:16:43.780 --> 00:16:44.960
Well, I'll show you then.

00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:45.380
Here.

00:16:45.850 --> 00:16:47.150
That's Patrick the Pup.

00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:49.670
He's a little item from FAO Schwartz.

00:16:49.770 --> 00:16:51.870
I thought she might like snuggling up to that.

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:53.010
How thoughtful.

00:16:53.590 --> 00:16:56.950
And, uh, you can throw away all those hand-me-downs she's been wearing.

00:16:57.870 --> 00:16:58.130
Here.

00:16:58.430 --> 00:16:59.980
I got a bacon.

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:09.000
dozen of these. They're Marie Guerin, a Parisian designer. She's the Lagerfeld of the Under-12

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:17.890
set. Wow. And the salesperson Sax told me that little girls still like their Barbies, so I got

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:23.900
this Queen of Hearts Barbie with a wardrobe designed by Bob Mackey. Kind of fetching,

00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:29.960
don't you think? Cool. There's a lot more stuff here, too. It's very...

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:36.330
generous, Maurice. Oh, it's entirely my pleasure. Is that Uncle Elvie thing? Well, it may sound

00:17:36.330 --> 00:17:40.010
strange, Shelly, but there's a lot of other people who believe in reincarnation, too. You know,

00:17:40.730 --> 00:17:46.170
George Patton always thought he was a soldier in Alexander's army at the Siege of Tyre. Maurice,

00:17:46.660 --> 00:17:52.140
I gotta tell you, Father Peron says reincarnation is bogus. Well, there's a billion Hindus that'll

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:57.240
argue that point with Father Peron all day long. You know, when I first visited Versailles about

00:17:57.240 --> 00:17:59.980
30 years ago, I had the strangest feeling, a kind of a...

00:18:00.280 --> 00:18:06.460
eerie familiarity about the place i don't know whether i was uh louis the 14th or marie antoinette

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:12.070
or maybe a lowly groundskeeper but i had lived there be that as it may maurice it's a little

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:17.460
far-fetched to think that randy is your uncle i wouldn't stake my life on it holling but uh

00:18:18.120 --> 00:18:23.380
humor me on this will you you have no idea how much uncle elvie meant to me the man taught me

00:18:23.380 --> 00:18:29.970
to read. Without him, I'd still be on the farm, whether Randy is or isn't.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:33.060
I plan to take a personal interest in the child from now on.

00:18:33.930 --> 00:18:36.720
Good night. Sleep tight?

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:44.340
So, we'll probably have to replace the rings and board.

00:18:45.460 --> 00:18:47.060
Yeah, probably gotta do a valve job.

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:50.840
So, you seen anybody?

00:18:52.440 --> 00:18:52.740
What?

00:18:54.520 --> 00:18:55.060
Seen anybody.

00:18:55.520 --> 00:18:56.360
You mean like a guy?

00:18:58.160 --> 00:18:58.420
Yeah.

00:18:59.600 --> 00:18:59.980
Hand me that.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.060
That ratchet with five-eats.

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:05.890
Okay, there you go.

00:19:07.210 --> 00:19:08.520
Uh, no.

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:09.360
No.

00:19:10.750 --> 00:19:14.800
My history with men.

00:19:15.450 --> 00:19:17.230
I just decided to give, you know,

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:19.950
the male-female thing a rest.

00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:21.700
Yeah, I can dig it.

00:19:26.680 --> 00:19:27.700
Why, why do you ask?

00:19:27.860 --> 00:19:28.200
No reason.

00:19:30.060 --> 00:19:39.270
His car puts the whole male-female thing into perspective, huh?

00:19:40.420 --> 00:19:41.040
It does.

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:46.780
Well, Cicely and Roslyn, they drive up here in that Model T, 1909.

00:19:47.920 --> 00:19:53.760
No Alcan, no Stuckey's, no roadside assistance.

00:19:56.560 --> 00:19:59.500
You throw a rod in that 2,000-mile stretch from Billings to here.

00:20:00.780 --> 00:20:01.220
Yeah.

00:20:03.160 --> 00:20:03.900
Well, they did it.

00:20:04.090 --> 00:20:06.070
No less than Hannibal crossed in the Alps.

00:20:06.330 --> 00:20:08.450
They did it themselves.

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:09.570
Men need not apply.

00:20:11.910 --> 00:20:12.190
See?

00:20:13.510 --> 00:20:13.750
Men.

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:15.450
Who needs them?

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:18.180
Who needs them?

00:20:21.620 --> 00:20:24.660
Well, how was that champagne?

00:20:25.340 --> 00:20:25.980
Oh, thank you.

00:20:26.100 --> 00:20:26.740
It was wonderful.

00:20:26.980 --> 00:20:27.200
Thanks.

00:20:27.740 --> 00:20:29.100
Listen, I gotta talk to you.

00:20:29.240 --> 00:20:29.980
You know, there's...

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:33.960
Something I intended to give you when we close, but I forgot.

00:20:34.390 --> 00:20:37.190
Seems that little piece of land of yours is kind of famous.

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:38.990
Oh?

00:20:39.860 --> 00:20:43.220
Claiborne, the representative from New Mexico,

00:20:43.540 --> 00:20:45.260
was fishing up there about ten years ago,

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:46.880
got caught in an avalanche.

00:20:47.530 --> 00:20:51.210
It took the search and rescue people two days to dig him out of there.

00:20:52.600 --> 00:20:55.160
A miraculous thing was that he was alive.

00:20:56.770 --> 00:20:58.130
Died on the way to the hospital.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:05.740
keep that if you'd like. Uh, thanks, Maurice. Actually, that's what I wanted to talk to you

00:21:05.740 --> 00:21:14.720
about, the property. Um, we've had second thoughts. It's a little late for that, isn't it? Well, I know

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:19.820
we've closed, and, you know, I'm assuming there'd be a penalty, of course, but Michelle and I were

00:21:19.820 --> 00:21:23.620
going over the finances, and it seems like we've bitten off a little more than we could chew right

00:21:23.620 --> 00:21:29.970
now, you know? Yeah, plus, there's the law.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:34.360
your issue of commitment. I'm not sure that we're ready to put down those kinds of roots

00:21:34.360 --> 00:21:39.850
yet. You know, I've always lived in big cities, and I'm having trouble visualizing myself

00:21:39.850 --> 00:21:48.970
as a country boy, you know? So, you know, you could just write out a check and deduct,

00:21:49.130 --> 00:21:57.890
of course, something for your trouble. That'd be fine. Well, it'd be the easiest thing in

00:21:57.890 --> 00:21:59.970
the world for me to return your money and take back the...

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:04.100
But it'd be a disservice to you.

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:06.860
No, no, no, it wouldn't.

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:10.950
You know, you remind me of myself when I first came up here.

00:22:11.640 --> 00:22:13.740
I went out and bought my first 800 acres.

00:22:14.300 --> 00:22:15.720
I leveraged myself to the max.

00:22:17.020 --> 00:22:19.380
I don't think I didn't feel some panic every once in a while,

00:22:19.520 --> 00:22:21.360
but it worked out fine for me, didn't it?

00:22:21.700 --> 00:22:24.120
Now, if I allowed you to crawl out of this deal,

00:22:25.690 --> 00:22:27.360
sometime, somewhere, you'd wonder

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:29.240
if you could have made it yourself.

00:22:40.040 --> 00:22:42.690
Listen, do you know, did we get any mail?

00:22:43.510 --> 00:22:47.120
No delivery today, Michelle. Red Murphy had to jettison his cargo.

00:22:47.890 --> 00:22:49.130
Jettison? He dumped the mail?

00:22:49.810 --> 00:22:55.560
His wings were icing. He was losing altitude. Cozy in that.

00:22:56.530 --> 00:22:58.050
You? It was you, Ruthann?

00:22:58.570 --> 00:22:58.990
What?

00:22:59.510 --> 00:22:59.970
You gave me...

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:06.960
this parka? Me? No. Somebody gave it to you? Yeah. Yeah, anonymously. Have you ever heard

00:23:06.960 --> 00:23:12.200
of anything so sneaky? Snooky? Well, obviously, the person was too much of a coward to tell

00:23:12.200 --> 00:23:17.140
me to my face that I stick out. You know, a city slicker. I don't think you should assume

00:23:17.140 --> 00:23:23.180
that. I know a lot of people who do nice things anonymously. You do? Mm-hmm. They just don't

00:23:23.180 --> 00:23:29.210
want any thanks. It makes them uncomfortable. Really? Mm-hmm. I remember last year, Rusty

00:23:29.210 --> 00:23:29.970
for that abortion.

00:23:30.700 --> 00:23:35.160
Every Thursday for three months, someone left a freshly killed animal on his porch.

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:38.360
Never did find out who the good Samaritan was.

00:23:39.220 --> 00:23:42.890
Huh. Well, maybe you're right.

00:23:43.920 --> 00:23:44.840
Yeah, thanks, Ruthann.

00:23:44.980 --> 00:23:45.420
You're welcome.

00:23:47.270 --> 00:23:50.770
Bernice, you left your hat. Bernice.

00:23:51.720 --> 00:23:54.240
Oh, Michelle, I thought you were Bernice Crandall.

00:23:54.460 --> 00:23:54.820
Nope.

00:23:55.540 --> 00:23:58.410
She left her hat. There she is.

00:24:02.290 --> 00:24:04.040
Bernice, don't forget your hat.

00:24:04.650 --> 00:24:05.610
Oh, thanks, Holling.

00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:15.940
Michelle?

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:17.410
In the air.

00:24:20.690 --> 00:24:22.690
You know, I've been thinking a lot about that prayer.

00:24:22.930 --> 00:24:25.110
You know that prayer you see on greeting cards all the time?

00:24:25.690 --> 00:24:29.620
Oh, God, give me the serenity to accept we cannot be changed.

00:24:30.600 --> 00:24:35.010
and the courage to change what can be changed and the wisdom to know the difference it's nice

00:24:35.010 --> 00:24:43.020
honey acceptance that's what's important i'm just gonna have to accept the fact that maurice

00:24:43.020 --> 00:24:53.090
is not going to take his line back what not important what are you doing i am packing these

00:24:53.090 --> 00:24:58.310
horrible clothes away and getting them out of my sight oh i don't care who gave them to me how good

00:24:58.310 --> 00:24:59.970
their intentions were maybe it was a mother

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:03.300
Teresa of the North, but let me tell you, fella, the road to hell is paved with good intentions.

00:25:03.760 --> 00:25:09.290
The square in the box?

00:25:13.110 --> 00:25:13.470
Shelly?

00:25:14.430 --> 00:25:14.930
Hey, H.

00:25:15.160 --> 00:25:17.320
Check out this gizmo Maurice gave Miranda.

00:25:18.520 --> 00:25:19.140
Show Daddy.

00:25:19.740 --> 00:25:23.060
Shelly, Randy's gonna get tired of that thing with all those gadgets and the noise.

00:25:23.360 --> 00:25:24.040
Look what I made her.

00:25:25.870 --> 00:25:27.170
Randy, look what Daddy made you.

00:25:28.010 --> 00:25:28.930
Yeah, I know.

00:25:29.210 --> 00:25:29.870
What shall we call it?

00:25:30.540 --> 00:25:31.640
Jimmy or Bunny?

00:25:33.920 --> 00:25:34.950
Oh, I'll get it.

